    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0271",
      "sequence": 283,
      "meaning": "Rāma says, ‘Lord, whoever broke Śambhu’s bow must be one of your servants. Why not command me and state your wish?’ The wrathful sage answers angrily that a servant performs service; one who acts like an enemy deserves battle. Whoever broke Śiva’s bow is his enemy like Sahasrabāhu and must separate himself from the assembly, or every king there will be killed. Hearing him, Lakṣmaṇa smiles and speaks mockingly to the axe-bearer: ‘We broke many bows in childhood, master, yet you never grew angry like this. Why such attachment to this bow?’ Enraged, the banner of Bhṛgu’s clan replies: ‘Royal boy, doomed by Time, you speak without heed. Is Tripurāri’s bow, renowned throughout the world, equal to ordinary bows?’",
