    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0272",
      "sequence": 284,
      "meaning": "Laughing, Lakṣmaṇa says, ‘As we understand it, sir, all bows are alike. What loss or gain comes from breaking an old bow? Rāma merely looked at it and touched it; it broke, so Raghupati too is blameless. Why be angry without cause?’ Paraśurāma looks toward his axe and replies: ‘Fool, have you not heard my nature? I refrain from killing you only because you are a boy; you take me for an ordinary witless sage. I am a fierce lifelong celibate, renowned throughout the world as an enemy of the warrior caste. By arm-strength I repeatedly made the earth kingless and gave it to Brahmins. Prince, behold this terrible axe that severed Sahasrabāhu’s arms: it destroys unborn children in the womb; do not plunge your parents into grief.’",
