    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0273",
      "sequence": 285,
      "meaning": "Lakṣmaṇa laughs and gently replies: ‘Ah, great sage, what a mighty warrior! Again and again you show me the axe, wishing to blow away a mountain with a puff. No tender gourd is here to die at the sight of a pointing finger. Seeing your axe, bow and arrows, I answered with some pride. Recognizing you as Bhṛgu’s son from the sacred thread, I restrain my anger and endure whatever you say. Our lineage does not display heroism against gods, Brahmins, devotees of Hari or cows: killing you would bring sin, defeat would bring disgrace, and even if you killed me I would fall at your feet. Your words equal ten million thunderbolts; you carry bow, arrows and axe in vain. Forgive anything improper I said on seeing them, steadfast great sage.’ Hearing this, the furious jewel of Bhṛgu’s line answers in a grave voice.",
