    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0274",
      "sequence": 286,
      "meaning": "Paraśurāma tells Kauśika: ‘Hear me—this dull boy is crooked, ruled by Death and bent on destroying his clan. He is a stain upon the solar line, utterly unbridled, ignorant and fearless. Within a moment he will be Time’s morsel; I declare it openly, and the fault will not be mine. Restrain him if you wish to save him, after telling him of my might, strength and anger.’ Lakṣmaṇa replies: ‘Sage, who could adequately recount your fine fame while you are present? With your own mouth you have narrated your deeds many times and in many ways. If you are not satisfied, say something more; do not suppress anger and bear unbearable pain. You are a courageous, steadfast and unruffled warrior under vows, yet abuse does not become you. Heroes perform deeds in battle; they do not advertise themselves. A coward, finding an enemy present on the field, merely recounts his prowess.’",
