    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0275",
      "sequence": 287,
      "meaning": "Lakṣmaṇa adds, ‘You seem to have brought Time at your command and keep summoning him against me.’ Hearing his harsh words, Paraśurāma readies the terrible axe in his hand: ‘Let people no longer blame me. This sharp-tongued boy deserves death. I have spared him because he is young, but now he has truly become one destined to die.’ Kauśika asks him to forgive the offence, for saints do not reckon a child’s faults. Paraśurāma answers, ‘I am merciless and wrathful, and this offender is a betrayer of his guru. I spare him without killing only because of your character, Kauśika; otherwise I would cut him down with this hard axe and cheaply repay my debt to my guru.’ Gādhi’s son laughs inwardly: the sage sees everything green—he still does not understand that this is a steel sword, not one made of sugarcane.",
